%I A369368 #19 Oct 21 2026 00:03:08
%S A369368 0,1,6,3,24,165,2550,10,3090,390,1296,265230
%N A369368 a(n) is the numerator of the maximum expected number of steps until a random walker falls into the sea when walking on the cells of an n-celled polyhex island.
%C A369368 This is the polyhex version of A366998.
%C A369368 For all n <= 11, the optimal island (with a designated starting cell) is unique up to translations, rotations, and reflections. See linked illustration.
%H A369368 Pontus von Brömssen, <a href="/A369368/a369368.svg">Illustration of the optimal islands and starting cells (black dots) for n = 1..11</a>.
%H A369368 Pontus von Brömssen, <a href="https://oeis.org/plot2a?name1=A369368&amp;name2=A369369&amp;tform1=untransformed&amp;tform2=untransformed&amp;shift=0&amp;radiop1=ratio&amp;drawpoints=true">Plot of a(n)/A369369(n) vs n</a>, using Plot2.
%H A369368 <a href="/index/Pol#polyominoes">Index entries for sequences related to polyominoes</a>.
%e A369368 For n = 0, the random walker falls into the sea immediately, so a(0) = 0.
%e A369368 For n = 1, the starting cell is the only cell of the island, so the random walker falls into the sea after the first step and a(1) = 1.
%e A369368 For n = 2, the island consists of the starting cell and one adjacent cell, so the probability that the random walker stays on the island is 1/6 at each step. This implies that the expected number of steps is 6/5, so a(2) = 6. (The number of steps follows a geometric distribution.)
%e A369368 For n = 3, the optimal island consists of three mutually adjacent cells. As for n = 2, the number of steps follows a geometric distribution, now with the probability 1/3 of surviving at each step, so the expected number of steps is 3/2 and a(3) = 3.
%e A369368 See linked illustration for optimal solutions for 1 <= n <= 11.
%Y A369368 Cf. A000228, A369369 (denominators), A366998 (polyominoes), A369370 (polyiamonds), A397795 (polyknights).
